    "created_at": "2017-09-08T13:35:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-09-08T14:16:58Z",
    "body": "I agree that having heading or sectioning content inside `<label>` isn't a good idea. But I don't see how allowing `<div>` (and probably `<p>`) inside it could harm anything.\r\n\r\nYes, any visual structure inside `<label>` can be done with `<span>`s and similar elements (including non-conforming, but still widely used practice of `<i>` for icons), but often it feels as kind of artificial limitation. There are examples of web application UI that require using block-level elements as clickable labels for form controls, and faking it by changing the CSS `display` property of `<span>`s or emulating it with JS doesn't seem a right way to do it.\r\n\r\nMaybe the content model of `<label>` could be consistent [with one of the `<th>` element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/tables.html#the-th-element) (minus nested labels and labelable elements other than the labeled control, obviously)?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
