    "created_at": "2017-09-08T15:52:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-09-08T15:52:31Z",
    "body": "All content models are \"artificial limitations\" in a sense. Fortunately, HTML has given you the perfect tool to meet this restriction: the twin elements of span vs. div. In this situation, span is more appropriate, so it's what should be used.\r\n\r\nUsing CSS to change a span a different display is perfectly fine; it's definitely the right way to do it. CSS is for taking care of such presentational concerns, once you've conformed to the content model by choosing the right element to represent whether this is a non-semantic wrapper element (div) or other non-semantic element (span).\r\n\r\nLet me close this, as it doesn't seem like the proposal here will enable authors to do anything new; we'd need something that's impossible to do with a span and that requires a div. Of course, if we come up with such a thing somehow, I'm happy to reopen or consider alternatives. I'm also happy to continue discussing in the closed thread.",
